Apple's upcoming iPhone 18e model will retain a 60Hz display but could gain an upgraded front-facing camera, according to the leaker known as "Digital Chat Station."<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/03/iphone-17e-notch.jpg" alt="" width="2000" height="1125" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1038249" /><br/>
In a <a href="https://weibo.com/6048569942/5358629608751441">new post</a> on Weibo, the leaker said that the device will feature a 6.12-inch <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/macbook-ultra/">OLED</a> display using low-temperature polycrystalline silicon (LTPS) technology, the A20 chip, and a single 48-megapixel rear camera. They were less definitive about the front-facing camera, saying it could be upgraded to 24 megapixels.<br/>
<br/>
The specifications suggest that the iPhone 18e will offer no major display or rear camera hardware upgrades over the previous two generations. Both the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/iphone-16e-vs-17e/">iPhone 16e and iPhone 17e</a> feature approximately 6.1-inch &zwnj;OLED&zwnj; displays with a 60Hz refresh rate and a single 48-megapixel rear camera. The post does not detail the rear camera's sensor or lens, so smaller hardware changes remain possible.<br/>
<br/>
The A20 chip would represent a more significant upgrade. Like the A20 Pro chip in the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-18-pro/">iPhone 18 Pro</a>, the A20 is expected to use a <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/03/22/kuo-a20-chip-2nm/">2nm manufacturing process</a>, bringing notable performance and power efficiency improvements over the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-17e/">iPhone 17e</a>'s <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/3nm/">3nm</a> A19 chip. It would also bring the device into line with the upcoming standard <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-18/">iPhone 18</a>, which is <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/06/26/iphone-18-9gb-ram/">expected to use the same chip</a>.<br/>
<br/>
The front-facing camera claim is less clear. Digital Chat Station specified 24 megapixels, although it is unclear whether this refers to a new camera or is a mistaken reference to the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/09/09/new-iphones-feature-18mp-front-camera/">18-megapixel Center Stage camera</a> introduced with the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-17/">iPhone 17</a> lineup. A 24-megapixel camera was <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/11/07/iphone-18-lineup-24mp-selfie-cameras/">widely rumored</a> before those devices launched. Either resolution would represent an upgrade over the 12-megapixel front camera used by the iPhone 16e and &zwnj;iPhone 17e&zwnj;.<br/>
<br/>
Digital Chat Station <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/06/29/iphone-18e-wont-get-promotion-display-says-leaker/">previously claimed</a> that the iPhone 18e would retain a 60Hz LTPS display. An earlier <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/03/17/iphone-19e-120hz-promotion-display/">report from Korea</a> suggested that an LTPO display capable of supporting 120Hz ProMotion could reach the entry-level iPhone with the "iPhone 19e" in 2028.<br/>
